1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,138
Day 17 of the war.

2
00:00:02,178 --> 00:00:05,776
The United States formally
asked its allies for

3
00:00:05,816 --> 00:00:09,259
military help to reopen
the Strait of Hormuz.

4
00:00:09,299 --> 00:00:10,867
France refused first.

5
00:00:10,907 --> 00:00:14,160
Germany, Italy, Spain
and Belgium followed.

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,538
China refused too.

7
00:00:15,578 --> 00:00:18,242
Within two hours the call was over,

8
00:00:18,282 --> 00:00:24,153
and President Trump issued a sharply
worded statement condemning the allies.

9
00:00:26,353 --> 00:00:32,497
South Korea, Japan and Australia also
declined to join the Hormuz coalition.

10
00:00:32,537 --> 00:00:36,445
All three cited constitutional
limits on sending

11
00:00:36,485 --> 00:00:40,146
forces abroad without
parliamentary approval.

12
00:00:40,186 --> 00:00:42,099
Seoul pledged only $500,

13
00:00:42,139 --> 00:00:47,550
000 in humanitarian aid and position
data for Korean-flagged ships.

14
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:53,286
Trump answered the allies with a
declaration: America would win alone.

15
00:00:55,486 --> 00:00:57,067
Day 18, before dawn.

16
00:00:57,107 --> 00:01:03,391
With U.S. cooperation, Israel struck
several senior Iranian officials at once.

17
00:01:03,431 --> 00:01:09,110
At 5:12 AM, Iranian state TV confirmed
the deaths of former parliament

18
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:14,175
speaker Ali Larijani and Basij
commander Gholamreza Soleimani.

19
00:01:14,215 --> 00:01:18,918
The oldest channel in Iran's
nuclear diplomacy had closed.

20
00:01:21,118 --> 00:01:25,752
Iran's retaliation began one hour
and forty-two minutes later.

21
00:01:25,792 --> 00:01:31,179
Ballistic missiles hit central Ramat
Gan, killing two Israeli civilians.

22
00:01:31,219 --> 00:01:36,456
That afternoon, Israel launched a
ground invasion of southern Lebanon.

23
00:01:36,496 --> 00:01:39,019
UNICEF announced that more than 1,

24
00:01:39,059 --> 00:01:43,583
100 children had been killed or
wounded since the war began.

25
00:01:45,783 --> 00:01:51,162
There were fourteen monitors, and
every one of them turned red at once.

26
00:01:51,202 --> 00:01:56,506
National Security Adviser Brian
Collins did not get up from his chair.

27
00:01:56,546 --> 00:02:02,231
The most dangerous moment in a war isn't
when everyone jumps to their feet.

28
00:02:02,271 --> 00:02:03,910
It's when no one does.

29
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:06,047
Confusion makes people move.

30
00:02:06,087 --> 00:02:08,032
Panic makes them go still.

31
00:02:08,072 --> 00:02:11,619
“NATO's out, sir. Japan
too. Korea. Australia.”

32
00:02:11,659 --> 00:02:13,757
His aide kept his voice low.

33
00:02:13,797 --> 00:02:15,589
Collins didn't even nod.

34
00:02:15,629 --> 00:02:17,955
An alliance was never a person.

35
00:02:17,995 --> 00:02:23,832
An alliance was a system, and systems
always flow toward their own interests.

36
00:02:23,872 --> 00:02:26,926
A war you start alone, you finish alone.

37
00:02:29,126 --> 00:02:34,578
Foreign Minister Lee Junhyeok stood
at the window, looking down at Seoul.

38
00:02:34,618 --> 00:02:39,168
People were walking to work,
knowing nothing. He envied them.

39
00:02:39,208 --> 00:02:43,005
The cable from the U.S.
Embassy was two lines long.

40
00:02:43,045 --> 00:02:45,940
A request to join the Hormuz operation.

41
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:49,250
An answer required within
twenty-four hours.

42
00:02:49,290 --> 00:02:52,712
The reply had already
gone out under his name.

43
00:02:52,752 --> 00:02:58,129
Formal regrets, constitutional
constraints, a strong call for diplomacy.

44
00:02:58,169 --> 00:03:01,139
“Tell them there's no official reaction.

45
00:03:01,179 --> 00:03:04,976
We support it, but we won't
take part. That's all.”

46
00:03:05,016 --> 00:03:09,229
When one nation leaves another
alone on the battlefield,

47
00:03:09,269 --> 00:03:13,142
it is always done in a
properly formatted document.

48
00:03:15,342 --> 00:03:17,383
The children were asleep.

49
00:03:17,423 --> 00:03:23,541
Fatima al-Rashid had put all three to
bed and sat down in the living room.

50
00:03:23,581 --> 00:03:27,286
Since the war began,
every sound had changed.

51
00:03:27,326 --> 00:03:30,033
An engine sounded like a missile.

52
00:03:30,073 --> 00:03:32,779
Lightning felt like an explosion.

53
00:03:32,819 --> 00:03:39,520
That night, an Iranian drone struck an
eight-story apartment building in Salmiya.

54
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:41,018
The ceiling shook.

55
00:03:41,058 --> 00:03:46,012
Her youngest was crying — five
years old, not even sure why.

56
00:03:46,052 --> 00:03:50,506
Something was frightening,
and that was reason enough.

57
00:03:50,546 --> 00:03:54,001
Fatima held her and looked out the window.

58
00:03:54,041 --> 00:03:57,247
Smoke rose from the building next door.

59
00:03:57,287 --> 00:03:58,995
There were no lights.

60
00:03:59,035 --> 00:04:01,741
Wars are fought between soldiers.

61
00:04:01,781 --> 00:04:08,606
But in the darkness a war leaves behind,
the ones who cry are always the children.

62
00:04:10,806 --> 00:04:13,476
Altitude: twelve thousand meters.

63
00:04:13,516 --> 00:04:16,925
The Israeli pilot checked the coordinates.

64
00:04:16,965 --> 00:04:18,731
Then he checked again.

65
00:04:18,771 --> 00:04:21,113
The target was not a soldier.

66
00:04:21,153 --> 00:04:27,436
But in war, the line between civilians
and the powerful exists only on paper.

67
00:04:27,476 --> 00:04:34,334
He thought of his mother in Haifa — the
woman who never wanted her son in this seat.

68
00:04:34,374 --> 00:04:39,590
The release button is pressed with
two fingers. That's the rule.

69
00:04:39,630 --> 00:04:41,561
One finger is a mistake.

70
00:04:41,601 --> 00:04:44,106
Two fingers make it a decision.

71
00:04:44,146 --> 00:04:46,242
There were two explosions.

72
00:04:46,282 --> 00:04:48,541
The first took off the roof.

73
00:04:48,581 --> 00:04:50,840
No one remembers the second.

74
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:54,822
Memory only reaches as
far as the moment before.

75
00:04:57,022 --> 00:05:00,521
The siren had sounded six minutes earlier.

76
00:05:00,561 --> 00:05:05,240
David Levy, seventy-two, did
not go down to the shelter.

77
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:08,442
He had lived through 1967, 1973, 2006.

78
00:05:08,482 --> 00:05:10,886
He had heard too many sirens.

79
00:05:10,926 --> 00:05:12,824
He stood at his window.

80
00:05:12,864 --> 00:05:17,964
In the early morning, Ramat Gan
glowed the color of a sunset.

81
00:05:18,004 --> 00:05:19,565
The glass trembled.

82
00:05:19,605 --> 00:05:23,188
The next moment, he was
lying on the floor.

83
00:05:23,228 --> 00:05:26,138
The sound had gone out of his ears.

84
00:05:26,178 --> 00:05:31,193
The ceiling was cracked, and
dust drifted down. This is war.

85
00:05:31,233 --> 00:05:34,058
Not because I didn't take shelter.

86
00:05:34,098 --> 00:05:37,092
Because the war came looking for me.

87
00:05:37,132 --> 00:05:39,367
Civilians never choose war.

88
00:05:39,407 --> 00:05:42,188
But war always chooses civilians.

89
00:05:44,388 --> 00:05:49,748
The treads of a Merkava tank were
far quieter than people imagine.

90
00:05:49,788 --> 00:05:51,794
Silent. Ominously silent.

91
00:05:51,834 --> 00:05:55,718
For Mohammad Kasser at a
Hezbollah lookout post,

92
00:05:55,758 --> 00:05:59,888
the tanks he couldn't see
were the most terrifying.

93
00:05:59,928 --> 00:06:04,582
If you could see them, at least
you knew which way to run.

94
00:06:04,622 --> 00:06:06,767
This was not an air strike.

95
00:06:06,807 --> 00:06:09,600
Air strikes came down from the sky.

96
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:14,132
This came across the ground.
Armor. Infantry. Artillery.

97
00:06:14,172 --> 00:06:15,994
The oldest form of war.

98
00:06:16,034 --> 00:06:17,856
He picked up his AK-47.

99
00:06:17,896 --> 00:06:19,960
He couldn't shoot the sky.

100
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,983
He couldn't stop a drone.

101
00:06:22,023 --> 00:06:24,250
A border is a line on a map.

102
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:28,822
Tank treads roll right over
it — and the line is erased.

103
00:06:31,022 --> 00:06:32,549
There was a number.

104
00:06:32,589 --> 00:06:34,611
More than eleven hundred.

105
00:06:34,651 --> 00:06:36,261
Two hundred in Iran.

106
00:06:36,301 --> 00:06:40,632
Ninety-one in Lebanon. Four
in Israel. One in Kuwait.

107
00:06:40,672 --> 00:06:45,745
The report had no names in it.
Only numbers. Numbers are tidy.

108
00:06:45,785 --> 00:06:47,807
Children's names are not.

109
00:06:47,847 --> 00:06:50,199
In Isfahan, Zahra was twelve.

110
00:06:50,239 --> 00:06:55,807
She fell on a stairwell while running
for shelter and hurt her head.

111
00:06:55,847 --> 00:06:58,529
It wasn't a missile. It was fear.

112
00:06:58,569 --> 00:07:00,838
In Nabatieh, Youssef is six.

113
00:07:00,878 --> 00:07:04,715
He is alive, but his right
ear no longer hears.

114
00:07:04,755 --> 00:07:08,261
Children don't know why
anyone is fighting.

115
00:07:08,301 --> 00:07:09,993
Adults think they do.

116
00:07:10,033 --> 00:07:13,497
That difference is what makes all of this.

117
00:07:15,697 --> 00:07:21,514
Lieutenant Kim Junho watched the screens
in the combat information center.

118
00:07:21,554 --> 00:07:27,766
A Korean Navy liaison officer, his job
was to send Seoul what he saw and heard.

119
00:07:27,806 --> 00:07:32,119
Larijani's name appeared on
the list of confirmed dead.

120
00:07:32,159 --> 00:07:38,134
“I hear Trump went after Korea too?”
asked U.S. Navy officer Mark Henderson.

121
00:07:38,174 --> 00:07:41,062
“No official reaction from our side.”

122
00:07:41,102 --> 00:07:43,437
Henderson gave a bitter laugh.

123
00:07:43,477 --> 00:07:44,861
“That's diplomacy.

124
00:07:44,901 --> 00:07:49,452
Saying nothing is still saying
something.” Kim didn't nod.

125
00:07:49,492 --> 00:07:55,546
On a carrier before dawn, he wasn't sure
saying nothing was really all right.

126
00:07:55,586 --> 00:07:57,603
Watching was a choice too.

127
00:07:57,643 --> 00:08:00,967
And every choice came with responsibility.

128
00:08:03,167 --> 00:08:05,610
In Tehran, Larijani was dead.

129
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:09,035
In Ramat Gan, two civilians were killed.

130
00:08:09,075 --> 00:08:12,973
In southern Lebanon,
tanks crossed the border.

131
00:08:13,013 --> 00:08:15,113
In Kuwait, a child cried.

132
00:08:15,153 --> 00:08:18,623
In Seoul, a minister looked out a window.

133
00:08:18,663 --> 00:08:24,571
And millions of people, watching the
news, turned off their screens —

134
00:08:24,611 --> 00:08:31,208
not because it was unbearable, but because
they had seen too much to feel it.

135
00:08:31,248 --> 00:08:34,930
Rumi's Masnavi begins:
“Listen to the reed,

136
00:08:34,970 --> 00:08:39,084
how it tells a tale,
complaining of separation.”

137
00:08:39,124 --> 00:08:44,563
A reed only makes a sound once
it's been torn from the reed bed.

138
00:08:44,603 --> 00:08:51,070
Whether that sound is a melody or a
scream depends on the ear that hears it.

139
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:54,665
The cruelest moment of war isn't the bomb.

140
00:08:54,705 --> 00:08:57,062
It's when we get used to it.

141
00:08:57,102 --> 00:08:58,689
— End of Episode 6.

142
00:08:58,729 --> 00:09:02,753
Continued in Episode 7,
“Fire at South Pars.” —
